cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you cite this protocol or the accompanying code, please use the metadata below."
title: "Three gates, three proxies: auditing the null-report checks of a pre-registered LLM-agent evaluation"
# This abstract must match the Abstract section of manuscript/main.md verbatim.
# analysis/scripts/check_claim_boundary.py compares them on every run, so editing
# one without the other fails repro.sh.
abstract: >-
  We audit the three gates by which one of our own pre-registered evaluations fixed what a null
  result would be worth. A language-model agent names which of five zones it moves to, and the
  evaluation asked whether a channel from its recent movement to its sampling shifts that choice by
  more than a total-variation margin of 0.10. Each gate ran as written and read a quantity other
  than the one its reading depends on. The entropy floor decided the outcome: it stopped the rules
  on the second of two models although each cell produced two to four zones, and a post hoc
  simulation on that model's channel-off draws finds that whether it stops a shift of the margin's
  size depends on the shift's direction. The other two gates did not change the outcome. The power
  gate reads a chi-square surrogate that puts the permutation test that decides at the surrogate's
  1.0 from half the margin upward, but at a tenth of the margin the surrogate returns 1.0 where the
  test rejects in 122 of 1,000 simulated replicates. The cap on draws with no recorded zone reads
  the worst cell, not the difference between conditions, which a later test put at 2.6 and 1.9
  percentage points in the two arms, confounded with execution order and sampling settings. The case
  motivates three diagnostics: simulate the power of the test that decides, report support beside
  entropy, and compare dropped outputs between conditions. The claims concern these gates on two
  models and one prompt, not the effect of the channel.
type: software
